WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.017
A recipe changes a site one step at a time.

00:00:04.017 --> 00:00:09.188
Orchard Core ships steps for features, themes, roles and content, and a module

00:00:09.188 --> 00:00:10.543
can add its own.

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:15.160
This video shows how a step handler is written, registered, and used.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.652
A recipe is a JSON file with a list of steps.

00:00:22.652 --> 00:00:24.296
Each step is an object.

00:00:24.296 --> 00:00:28.265
Its name says which step it is, and its other properties are the

00:00:28.265 --> 00:00:30.080
data that step works with.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:34.509
To run a recipe, Orchard Core takes its steps in order.

00:00:34.509 --> 00:00:39.394
It evaluates the script expressions in a step's values, then hands the step

00:00:39.394 --> 00:00:41.082
to every step handler.

00:00:41.082 --> 00:00:44.580
A handler acts only when the step's name is its own.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.796
The same handlers run wherever Orchard Core runs a recipe:

00:00:48.796 --> 00:00:53.129
on the Recipes page under Tools, during setup, in a deployment import, and

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:54.537
in a data migration.

00:00:54.537 --> 00:00:56.920
A step you write works in all of them.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.552
A step handler implements I Recipe Step Handler, which has one method, Execute Async.

00:01:06.552 --> 00:01:11.148
Orchard Core calls it for every step, passing a context with the step's

00:01:11.148 --> 00:01:14.610
Name, the Step as a JSON object, and an Errors list.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.664
Named Recipe Step Handler does the name check for you.

00:01:18.664 --> 00:01:22.905
Pass the step's name to its constructor, and it calls Handle Async only

00:01:22.905 --> 00:01:24.667
for a step with that name.

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:26.540
The comparison ignores case.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.716
The Themes step is a complete example.

00:01:29.716 --> 00:01:34.071
It derives from Named Recipe Step Handler, passes Themes as its name, and

00:01:34.071 --> 00:01:37.710
receives the services it works with through its constructor.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.066
Handle Async reads the step into a model with To Object.

00:01:42.066 --> 00:01:46.835
The properties of the model match the properties of the step, ignoring case.

00:01:46.835 --> 00:01:50.150
The handler then passes the values to its services.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.546
Orchard Core evaluates script expressions before any handler runs.

00:01:58.546 --> 00:02:02.276
By the time To Object reads the step, a value written as a

00:02:02.276 --> 00:02:08.220
JavaScript expression has been replaced by its result, so the handler never sees the expression.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.695
When the step's data does not fit a fixed class, read the JSON node yourself.

00:02:13.695 --> 00:02:18.306
The Shortcode Templates step calls Try Get Property Value on the step, then

00:02:18.306 --> 00:02:21.950
visits each property, whose key is the name of a template.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:29.072
A handler is not called until it is registered.

00:02:29.072 --> 00:02:33.373
In the Startup class of your module, call Add Recipe Execution Step with

00:02:33.373 --> 00:02:36.130
the handler type, as the Themes module does.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.084
The extension method adds the handler to the services of the tenant as

00:02:41.084 --> 00:02:43.131
a scoped I Recipe Step Handler.

00:02:43.131 --> 00:02:48.110
Being scoped, the handler's constructor can ask for any service the tenant provides.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:53.175
A Startup class without a Feature attribute belongs to the feature of the

00:02:53.175 --> 00:02:57.054
module itself, so the step exists only on tenants that enable it.

00:02:57.054 --> 00:03:00.550
A step that no handler claims is skipped without an error.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.728
A recipe is a file in the Recipes folder of a module or theme.

00:03:08.728 --> 00:03:13.466
Its top-level properties name and describe it, and each object in the steps

00:03:13.466 --> 00:03:17.210
list is a step, spelled with the name its handler expects.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.458
On the Recipes page under Tools, Orchard Core lists the recipes that are

00:03:22.458 --> 00:03:25.687
not setup recipes and do not carry the hidden tag.

00:03:25.687 --> 00:03:29.550
Run executes a recipe, and a notification reports the result.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:38.085
To report a problem, add a message to the Errors list of the context.

00:03:38.085 --> 00:03:42.073
The Recipes step does this when a recipe is not found, with the

00:03:42.073 --> 00:03:46.350
string localizer it receives, and then continues with the next item.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.671
When a step ends with errors, Orchard Core marks it as failed and

00:03:50.671 --> 00:03:52.129
stops the recipe there.

00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:53.940
The steps after it do not run.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.996
On the Recipes page, the notification says the recipe failed to run due

00:03:58.996 --> 00:04:02.127
to the following errors, then lists your messages.

00:04:02.127 --> 00:04:06.097
Write each one for the person who runs the recipe, as a localized

00:04:06.097 --> 00:04:08.110
string without sensitive data.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:13.773
A thrown exception reaches the notification only as a general message that names

00:04:13.773 --> 00:04:16.401
the step, and its details go to the log.

00:04:16.401 --> 00:04:21.200
Add to the Errors list for anything the person who runs the recipe can act on.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.887
Named Recipe Step Handler suits a class that handles one step name.

00:04:29.887 --> 00:04:35.234
To handle several names, implement I Recipe Step Handler directly and compare the

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:38.430
names yourself, as the Translations step does.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:46.981
To write a step, derive from Named Recipe Step Handler with the step's

00:04:46.981 --> 00:04:51.619
name, read the step with To Object, and report problems in the Errors list.

00:04:51.619 --> 00:04:54.940
Register the handler with Add Recipe Execution Step.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:58.854
Then spell the step's name in the steps of a recipe.

00:04:58.854 --> 00:05:04.037
A separate video covers deployment steps, which contribute the recipe steps that handlers

00:05:04.037 --> 00:05:05.510
like this one import.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.645
That is how a recipe step works:

00:05:09.645 --> 00:05:13.745
a handler that owns a name, a registration in Startup, and a recipe

00:05:13.745 --> 00:05:15.150
that uses the name.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:16.610
Thanks for watching.

